# Explore Determinant Geometry

A $2\times2$ matrix turns a unit square into a parallelogram. Its determinant is the signed area, while its square energy is the sum of the squares of all four entries. Begin by changing the matrix yourself:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The experiment illustrates the sharp law $2|\det M|\le E(M)$. Equality means the columns are perpendicular and equally long.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the geometric proof</summary>
The determinant magnitude is at most the product of the column lengths. Twice that product is at most the sum of their squared lengths. Equality in both steps requires perpendicular columns of equal length.
</details>

## The hidden quadratic form

A linear coordinate change transforms the determinant into $x_1^2+x_2^2-x_3^2-x_4^2$. Explore how positive and negative square energies cancel:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the coordinate transformation</summary>
Set $x_1=(a+d)/2$, $x_2=(b-c)/2$, $x_3=(a-d)/2$, and $x_4=(b+c)/2$. Expanding shows $ad-bc=x_1^2+x_2^2-x_3^2-x_4^2$. This is an indefinite quadratic form of signature $(2,2)$. For background, see [quadratic forms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadratic_form) and the [determinant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinant).
</details>

## Scaling and lattice arithmetic

Dilation by $r$ multiplies both energy and the two-dimensional determinant by $r^2$.

