<details><summary>Why the asymptotic exponent is $d(d-1)$</summary>
Matrix space has dimension $d^2$, so a radius-$T$ ball has volume on the scale $T^{d^2}$. The determinant has degree $d$; keeping it inside a fixed window costs a factor $T^{-d}$. The resulting scale is $T^{d^2-d}=T^{d(d-1)}$. Turning this scaling argument into a lattice asymptotic additionally requires quantitative distribution hypotheses. See the [geometry of numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geometry_of_numbers) for broader context.
</details>

